WEBVTT

NOTE Two kinds of not knowing

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.222
Some of what we do not know is chance. Some is ignorance.

2
00:00:04.222 --> 00:00:07.333
A forecast has to say which kind it faces.

3
00:00:07.933 --> 00:00:09.099
Roll a fair die.

4
00:00:09.099 --> 00:00:14.562
No one can say what comes up next, and more study of the die will not help.

5
00:00:14.562 --> 00:00:19.733
Engineers call this aleatory uncertainty, from the Latin word for dice.

6
00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:22.413
Now seal a number in an envelope.

7
00:00:22.413 --> 00:00:25.937
You do not know it either, but it is already fixed.

8
00:00:25.937 --> 00:00:28.701
Open the envelope and the doubt is gone.

9
00:00:28.701 --> 00:00:34.367
This is epistemic uncertainty: a gap in what we know, which more data could close.

10
00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:37.646
Which kind we face depends on the model.

11
00:00:37.646 --> 00:00:43.476
Two engineers put it plainly in 2009: "It is the job of the model builder to make

12
00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:44.700
the distinction."

13
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:49.197
A second question is whether a chance can be measured at all.

14
00:00:49.197 --> 00:00:53.093
In 1921 the economist Frank Knight pointed to fire insurance.

15
00:00:53.093 --> 00:00:56.031
No one can say whether one building will burn.

16
00:00:56.031 --> 00:01:00.567
Across many buildings, the loss evens out, and an insurer can carry it.

17
00:01:00.967 --> 00:01:04.603
A new business venture has no such group of similar cases.

18
00:01:04.603 --> 00:01:09.681
Knight called the measurable kind risk, and the kind that cannot be measured true

19
00:01:09.681 --> 00:01:10.433
uncertainty.

20
00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:15.183
In 1937, John Maynard Keynes drew a similar line, with examples.

21
00:01:15.183 --> 00:01:18.425
Roulette, he wrote, is not uncertain in his sense.

22
00:01:18.425 --> 00:01:23.676
The length of a life is only slightly uncertain, and the weather only moderately.

23
00:01:23.676 --> 00:01:28.733
A European war is uncertain, and so is the price of copper twenty years ahead.

24
00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:34.652
About these matters, he wrote, "there is no scientific basis on which to form any

25
00:01:34.652 --> 00:01:38.467
calculable probability whatever. We simply do not know."

26
00:01:39.267 --> 00:01:42.717
In 1961 Daniel Ellsberg turned this into a bet.

27
00:01:42.717 --> 00:01:48.885
On the left, Urn One holds a hundred red and black balls, in a mix nobody tells you.

28
00:01:48.885 --> 00:01:52.556
On the right, Urn Two holds exactly fifty of each.

29
00:01:52.556 --> 00:01:55.933
Draw your color and you win a hundred dollars.

30
00:01:56.333 --> 00:02:01.777
Most people would rather draw from Urn Two, whether they bet on red or on black.

31
00:02:01.777 --> 00:02:07.153
That choice treats red in Urn One as less likely than a half, and black as less

32
00:02:07.153 --> 00:02:11.100
likely too. The two chances would add up to less than one.

33
00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:14.497
No single probability fits those choices.

34
00:02:14.497 --> 00:02:20.219
The name for this is ambiguity: the probabilities themselves are unknown, or only

35
00:02:20.219 --> 00:02:25.800
partly known. In Urn One, the chance of red could be anything from zero to one.

36
00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:31.272
These kinds lie along one scale, from complete certainty to total ignorance.

37
00:02:31.272 --> 00:02:34.871
Choosing a supermarket line sits near the measured end.

38
00:02:34.871 --> 00:02:40.367
Keeping an umbrella in the car, for rain you cannot put odds on, sits further along.

39
00:02:40.767 --> 00:02:46.198
At the far end is total ignorance, where, in one team's words, "we do not even know

40
00:02:46.198 --> 00:02:51.433
that we do not know." A forecast should first say where on this scale it stands.
